00:00:10.809 --> 00:00:15.190
Hello and welcome to my video
on hemiacetals and acetals for

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.240
introductory organic chemistry.

00:00:17.799 --> 00:00:22.430
So hemiacetals occur when an
alcohol adds to a carbonyl group.

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:26.455
And this can be one of the trickiest
reactions that allied health students

00:00:26.455 --> 00:00:30.345
come across in their introductory
organic chemistry classes.

00:00:30.655 --> 00:00:33.925
So i'm going to try my best to
try to break it down for you in

00:00:33.925 --> 00:00:38.665
a very simple manner of really
what is occurring in the reaction.

00:00:39.005 --> 00:00:41.495
So it all starts from
just a simple carbonyl.

00:00:41.554 --> 00:00:46.214
So a carbonyl is anytime you have a
C double bonded to an oxygen and this

00:00:46.214 --> 00:00:48.324
can come in the many different forms.

00:00:48.345 --> 00:00:51.375
The specifically in this
reaction is whenever you have

00:00:51.375 --> 00:00:53.820
an aldehyde and a ketone.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:58.879
So whenever we have this carbonyl, it's
going to be reacting with an alcohol.

00:00:58.879 --> 00:01:02.660
So if you remember from class,
that is an OH group that's attached

00:01:02.669 --> 00:01:08.130
to some sort of R group, which
is an alkyl group or alkyl chain.

00:01:08.829 --> 00:01:12.770
So when these two things meet,
what is really going to happen?

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:16.010
So let's try to dive into
it and go from step by step.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.770
So if it all goes, let's
start with just an aldehyde.

00:01:20.075 --> 00:01:25.935
Reacting with an alcohol and what
this is going to make is a hemiacetal

00:01:26.425 --> 00:01:30.175
So if I take a aldehyde and an
alcohol, let's draw those out.

00:01:30.854 --> 00:01:34.705
So if I take the C double bond O So
we have our carbonyl now What makes an

00:01:34.714 --> 00:01:38.724
aldehyde is that it has a little hydrogen
on it and then that's going to be attached

00:01:38.735 --> 00:01:43.850
to an R group And this is going to be
coming into contact with an alcohol.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.789
So in order to show what exactly is
happening in this reaction, well we have

00:01:48.789 --> 00:01:53.070
to try to remember electronegativity,
which comes from introductory chemistry

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.120
and really how all these different atoms
can have these little partial negative,

00:01:57.169 --> 00:01:58.949
partial positive charges on them.

00:01:59.309 --> 00:02:03.199
So if you can remember elements
like oxygen, nitrogen, fluorine,

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:06.070
these are always considered to
be slightly electronegative.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.820
So they're always slightly negative.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.910
So why don't we put that
little delta negative on there.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.610
And then if you kind of remember
anything, anytime you have something

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:18.390
that's a little electronegative next
to something not so negative, well,

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:19.459
they're always going to be positive.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:24.489
So things like hydrogen and carbon in
this case will be a little positive.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:28.670
So, if you remember from intermolecular
forces, well, if I ever had a

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:32.705
hydrogen next to an oxygen, Well,
they're going to be making a

00:02:32.735 --> 00:02:34.665
connection or an intermolecular force.

00:02:34.995 --> 00:02:38.145
Same thing with this other oxygen
and carbon down here, right?

00:02:38.145 --> 00:02:40.225
They're going to be
attracted to one another.

00:02:40.765 --> 00:02:44.835
So when you have multiple attractions,
there's always a possibility for

00:02:44.865 --> 00:02:49.755
bonds to create, especially when
it's favorable for the structure.

00:02:49.845 --> 00:02:54.725
So this is how a hemiacetal
starts to form, okay?

00:02:56.225 --> 00:02:58.955
So this is where the hemiacetal is
going to come into play and what

00:02:58.955 --> 00:03:05.275
we're going to be doing is we're
going to be breaking two bonds and

00:03:05.275 --> 00:03:08.215
we're going to be forming two bonds.

00:03:08.975 --> 00:03:10.984
So, pal, let's show that.

00:03:10.995 --> 00:03:13.394
So, for breaking, what we're
going to do is we're going to

00:03:13.395 --> 00:03:15.124
show that with little red X's.

00:03:15.625 --> 00:03:19.664
And then for forming, we're going to be
showing that with little purple arrows.

00:03:20.195 --> 00:03:24.405
So the red X, what we'll see is we're
going to be breaking one of the carbon

00:03:24.405 --> 00:03:26.255
oxygen bonds, notice how there's two.

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:29.409
from the double bond and then
we're also going to be breaking

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:31.239
the OH bond that is here.

00:03:31.799 --> 00:03:34.179
So both those are going to break
and what they're going to do, it's

00:03:34.179 --> 00:03:37.509
going to essentially, it's going
to be rotating around the molecule.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.950
So what we're going to be doing
is we're going to show where

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.329
these new bonds are going to form.

00:03:43.749 --> 00:03:47.699
So the arrows are showing where
that's going to be rotating around.

00:03:47.865 --> 00:03:48.575
the molecule.

00:03:49.125 --> 00:03:53.985
So, when that happens, we're going
to take this intermediate structure

00:03:53.985 --> 00:03:58.675
that we have here, we're going to
copy and paste it down here, and show

00:03:58.925 --> 00:04:00.054
how everything's going to be broken.

00:04:00.055 --> 00:04:03.665
So, if everything with the red x's
is going to break, oops, we're going

00:04:03.665 --> 00:04:07.834
to show that going away, little blue
dots are going to go away in the

00:04:07.835 --> 00:04:12.885
process, and they're going to now be
replaced with little purple lines.

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:14.350
from before.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:18.200
Okay, so that shows that, all
right, those little purple arrows,

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.250
they moved and they rotated.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:22.890
So this is what we refer
to as our hemiacetal.

00:04:24.119 --> 00:04:27.379
So we have our hemiacetal
structure that is here.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:32.670
We can get rid of these little delta
pluses and minuses that we have here.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.020
And we can show our
intermediate structure.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:38.859
Now it looks a little funny, but that's
going to be the same thing as if I like,

00:04:38.859 --> 00:04:40.549
let's just redraw it for ourselves.

00:04:40.599 --> 00:04:47.280
So if we have a carbon with our
O H on one side and notice how we

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.880
have two oxygens connected to the
same carbon with a little hydrogen.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:54.320
And then an R group.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:59.099
Okay, so we have the little alkyl
groups that are attached here and it

00:04:59.099 --> 00:05:03.010
has that signature like little googly
eyes look to it in its structure.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:06.070
So this, though, is not the final step.

00:05:06.169 --> 00:05:08.789
Hemiacetals are very unstable.

00:05:09.429 --> 00:05:12.520
They tend to not want to be in
the structure all the time and the

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.030
reason behind that has to do with
with this little alcohol here.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:19.060
It's something, as you kind of
noticed in the last step, caused the

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:20.469
whole reaction in the first place.

00:05:20.469 --> 00:05:23.969
Well, the reaction is going to continue
because it wants to keep going.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.110
So how do we get to the final step?

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:32.180
Well, it has to go from a
hemiacetal to an acetal.

00:05:33.969 --> 00:05:38.290
So if this was, if we had step
one, we have our acetal here.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:45.580
Now for step two, what we're going to
be showing is a condensation Reaction.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:51.820
So in condensation, the whole idea is
that we're going to be Removing water.

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:55.929
So how do we remove water?

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:59.670
Well, whenever that is removed That's
something that's going to show that a

00:05:59.679 --> 00:06:04.990
bond is going to be created So let's
take our hemiacetal from before And

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:07.840
what we're now going to do is we're
going to copy and paste it down here

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:12.250
And being that this is in a solution of
alcohol being an alcohol reacted with

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:17.635
this earlier We're going to now show
how we're going to be removing Water.

00:06:17.955 --> 00:06:23.545
So if I draw an alcohol, so I have my OH
attached to an R group, and I'm going to

00:06:23.594 --> 00:06:26.594
color in my H from my other structure.

00:06:26.675 --> 00:06:29.785
Maybe you might see like, all
right, well, how is H2O removed?

00:06:30.254 --> 00:06:35.875
Well, we have H2O that is right
here, and we can see that that's

00:06:35.894 --> 00:06:39.000
going to be removed In the process.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:42.610
So when that happens, it's almost
like taking a straw to a liquid

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:44.290
and sucking the liquid out of it.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:47.330
And as a result, it's going to
vacuum and close it all together

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:49.489
and push it all closer together.

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:55.810
So when we have that, we're
going to redraw our structure.

00:06:56.609 --> 00:06:57.609
Just like so.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:02.190
And what we can now see is that
instead that purple line is now

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:07.480
going to be connected to the R group,
which was by that water earlier.

00:07:08.099 --> 00:07:11.460
And the other part of the structure
is going to stay the same.

00:07:13.090 --> 00:07:18.930
So now we have our signature look here
of two oxygens, so that's a little googly

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:20.860
eyes, both connected to the same carbon.

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:24.470
So we have, so if we want to
redraw that for ourselves, we can.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:29.630
So we have our carbon with an oxygen,
two of them both pointing in different

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:34.650
directions, with an, with different
R groups potentially on each side.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:39.900
Okay, so we have that look to
it, and this is what we refer

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:42.620
to as an acetal, all right?

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:47.519
So acetal is when it originates
from an aldehyde versus a ketal

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:50.889
is when it is from an aldehyde.

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:53.280
Okay, so let's sum all that up.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:54.799
So that way we have it in one location.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:58.610
What exactly is changing at
each of these different parts?

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:00.240
Well, let's go through it and try to see.

00:08:00.819 --> 00:08:04.830
So just in summary, what
functional groups are changing?

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:07.259
Because this is one of those
different mechanisms you can

00:08:07.499 --> 00:08:08.989
try to memorize for yourself.

00:08:08.989 --> 00:08:11.809
So that way when you get to
biochemistry, and you're trying to

00:08:11.809 --> 00:08:15.199
understand how carbohydrates form
with each other, we can try to

00:08:15.199 --> 00:08:16.609
remember the different groups we had.

00:08:17.069 --> 00:08:19.739
So if you remember in the beginning,
we started with a carbonyl.

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:23.760
That carbonyl, what
exactly did it turn into?

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:29.219
Well, if we go back up, we can see
that, okay, well, we had our carbonyl

00:08:29.649 --> 00:08:31.260
here that I'm highlighting in blue.

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:34.569
And we can see that it
turned into an alcohol.

00:08:34.809 --> 00:08:36.049
So that was step one.

00:08:36.559 --> 00:08:39.339
The alcohol, the carbonyl, turned into...

00:08:40.945 --> 00:08:41.554
and alcohol.

00:08:41.554 --> 00:08:45.585
So why don't we draw that for
ourselves, let that step happen.

00:08:46.345 --> 00:08:48.485
Then what happened to the
alcohol in the next step?

00:08:48.485 --> 00:08:49.725
Well, let's try to follow it.

00:08:50.045 --> 00:08:54.444
So if you remember from up here,
right, if this part was the part in

00:08:54.445 --> 00:09:01.500
blue, as we can see from above, well,
that's when it turned into An ether.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:07.160
So that entire step went from,
so in blue, went from carbonyl

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:10.680
to an alcohol to an ether.

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:13.920
Alright, what about the
other functional group?

00:09:13.939 --> 00:09:15.289
What does happen with the alcohol?

00:09:15.569 --> 00:09:17.150
Why don't we show that
one in a different color?

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:19.630
Why don't we pick um, let
